    explanation: "This is a useful interview question because it exposes the candidate's ability to reason about LRU/cache in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: Amazon, Microsoft, Joveo, OnlineSales AI, Flipkart, OLX Groups and other interview experiences. Sources: - LeetCode Amazon SDE1 onsite - LeetCode Microsoft SDE - LeetCode Joveo SDE1 - LeetCode OnlineSales AI SDE2 - GeeksforGeeks Flipkart SDE II - GeeksforGeeks OLX Groups Interview variants reported by Joveo SDE1, Microsoft SDE, OnlineSales AI SDE2, Flipkart SDE II, OLX Groups and other reports include O(1) get/put, a doubly linked list + hash map, and implementing it on paper.",
    sampleAnswer: "The standard LRU design uses two structures together: a hash map for fast key lookup and a doubly linked list for recency order. The map points to list nodes. On get(), move the node to the front; on put(), update or insert at the front and remove the tail when capacity is exceeded. Lookup, unlink, insert, and eviction are O(1) on average, with O(capacity) space.\n\nA concise implementation is shown in the detailed answer.",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nThe standard LRU design uses two structures together: a hash map for fast key lookup and a doubly linked list for recency order. The map points to list nodes. On get(), move the node to the front; on put(), update or insert at the front and remove the tail when capacity is exceeded. Lookup, unlink, insert, and eviction are O(1) on average, with O(capacity) space.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nfrom collections import OrderedDict\n\nclass LRUCache:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.capacity = capacity\n        self.cache = OrderedDict()\n\n    def get(self, key):\n        if key not in self.cache:\n            return -1\n        self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        return self.cache[key]\n\n    def put(self, key, value):\n        if key in self.cache:\n            self.cache.move_to_end(key)\n        self.cache[key] = value\n        if len(self.cache) > self.capacity:\n            self.cache.popitem(last=False)\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nWith capacity 2, put(1,A), put(2,B), get(1), then put(3,C) evicts key 2 because key 1 was just accessed. The example is deliberately focused on the specific interview topic in entry 7.",
