    explanation: "The important signal here is not just whether the candidate knows the pattern, but whether they can reason about stacks and queues in a way that is both correct and explainable. A strong response should make the core invariant, the important edge cases, and the complexity visible so the interviewer can distinguish genuine understanding from memorized code. The supplied source attributes this item to Reported by: OLX Groups. Source: GeeksforGeeks OLX Groups Interview Experience.",
    sampleAnswer: "Use a second stack that stores the minimum value seen at each depth. On push, push min(value, currentMinimum) onto the minimum stack; on pop, remove from both stacks. Then getMin() simply reads the top of the minimum stack. All three operations are O(1), with O(n) extra space.\n\nA concise implementation is shown in the detailed answer.",
    detailedAnswer: "Direct answer:\n\nUse a second stack that stores the minimum value seen at each depth. On push, push min(value, currentMinimum) onto the minimum stack; on pop, remove from both stacks. Then getMin() simply reads the top of the minimum stack. All three operations are O(1), with O(n) extra space.\n\n**Implementation example:**\n\n```python\nclass MinStack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.data = []\n        self.mins = []\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.data.append(x)\n        self.mins.append(x if not self.mins else min(x, self.mins[-1]))\n\n    def pop(self):\n        self.mins.pop()\n        return self.data.pop()\n\n    def getMin(self):\n        return self.mins[-1]\n```\n\n**Example:**\n\nFor the tree -10 with children 9 and 20, and 20 with children 15 and 7, the maximum path is 15 -> 20 -> 7 with sum 42. The example is deliberately focused on the specific interview topic in entry 23.",
